<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout xml:id="pp-7">
    <title>Portfolio Problem 7</title>

  <p>
    All problems are in an Overleaf document at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3tn"/>. Copy/paste problems
    from there to learn notation in Overleaf!

    The writing guidelines are at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3to"/>.

    There's a sample portfolio solution at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3v0"/>.

    There's an Overleaf Cheat Sheet at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3OL"/>.

    All of the writing guidelines are at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3NG"></url>.

    All of the proof portfolio objectives are at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3QC"></url>.
  </p>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Instructions:</title>
  <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Start with examples!
                    This is a logically complicated statement, so think about how to
                    do examples that ``push on'' the ``or'' and the ``if and only
                    if''.
                    Include these examples in both your rough draft and typed
                    revision.
                </p>
            </li>
  
            <li>
                <p>
                    If the conjecture is true, make it a theorem statement.
                    If the conjecture is false, modify the conjecture to make it
                    true.
                </p>
            </li>
  
            <li>
                <p>
                    Think about proof techniques!
                    You might get a lot of the proof portfolio objectives in this
                    problem.
                </p>
            </li>
  
            <li>
                <p>
                    In your typed revision, put a special focus on communicating
                    proof techniques you are using clearly.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
</paragraphs>


<paragraphs>
  <title>Conjecture 7</title>
  <p>
        Suppose <m>m,n\in\mathbb{Z}</m>.
        The expression <m>m^{2}(n^{2}+5)</m> is even if and only if <m>m</m> is even
        or <m>n</m> is odd.
    </p>
</paragraphs>
</handout>